< Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of the reasoning tokens goes in that decision. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5--7 bids with empirical win curves from eBay prices, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, scored on held-out auctions against an oracle calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Splitting the remaining loss, arithmetic slips are a large share for one model only; the rest comes at the end of the trace. Traces run out of tokens before every option is compared, or the final answer passes over the best option the trace computed, usually for the runner-up, in a way that follows list position within the same scenario. The information for the right bid is usually in the trace by 768 tokens: a lightweight verifier that reads it out recovers 77--100\% of the lead with the same tokens.
