< \emph{Utility ratio} $q=U(\hat b)/\max_b U(b)$ with expected profit $U(b)=p(b)(v-b)$ under held-out probabilities $p$. The oracle calculator returns $\arg\max_b \hat p(b)(v-b)$ and scores $q=0.981$; random choice scores 0.711. \emph{Gap recovered} at budget $c$ is $(q(c)-q(0))/(q_{\text{calc}}-q(0))$; the \emph{dollar gap} uses $U$ instead of $q$. \emph{Break-even} (B/e) is the smallest best expected profit per decision at which 768 tokens pay for themselves at rental prices.
