< \paragraph{Answers pass over the best option by list position.}
< Each scenario appears in three option orders, so the list position of the best option varies within a scenario. A conditional logit with scenario fixed effects shows that moving the best option from first to last raises the odds of passing over it 12-fold for Phi-mini and 3--35-fold for the Qwen models ($p\le0.01$), lowers them to 0.06 for Granite-8B ($p<0.001$), and has no significant effect for DeepSeek-8B. When the answer passes over the best option it usually takes the runner-up (29--68\% of cases) or the highest-probability bid (19--56\%); Phi-mini's choice is the first-listed option in 38\% of these cases and Granite-8B's the last-listed in 28\%. The model's free-text answer names the calculator's bid in at most 25\% of these traces, so this is not an artifact of the letter readout.
---
> \paragraph{Answers miss the best option by list position.}
> Each scenario appears in three option orders, so the list position of the best option varies within a scenario. In complete traces, a conditional logit with scenario fixed effects shows that moving the best option from first to last raises the odds of passing over it for Phi-mini (12-fold [7, 21], 369 events) and the Qwen models (3.4 [1.3, 8.8], 35 [6, 197] and 14 [3, 54] for 4B, 8B and 14B; 52--85 events), lowers them for Granite-8B (0.06 [0.03, 0.13], 221 events), and has no clear effect for DeepSeek-8B (0.67 [0.28, 1.6]). The direction differs by model, as in multiple-choice benchmarks \cite{zheng2024mcq}. Passed-over answers are mostly the runner-up (29--68\%) or the highest-probability bid (19--56\%), and repeat the model's own budget-zero answer in 32--61\% of cases, against 17\% by chance and 21--33\% for correct answers. The free-text answer names the calculator's bid in at most 25\% of these traces, so this is not an artifact of the letter readout.
