> \paragraph{A text reader recovers most of the rest.}
> The text reader recovers 72--97\% of the gap at 768 tokens from the same tokens, against 42--85\% for the model's answer, and 66--88\% at 512 tokens, where the answer recovers 17--56\% (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). It never lowers $q$ by more than 0.025 at any budget, and the stricter rule that waits for $K-1$ values never by more than 0.028. It gains most for Phi-mini (42\% to 91\%), whose traces finish but whose answers do not follow them, and least for Granite-8B (65\% to 72\%), where 38\% of the gap is options never reached. The oracle reader bounds what reading can add (81--100\%). Reading the trace's stated conclusion with our patterns does worse than the model's answer for five models. Sampling is no substitute for length: majority votes (ties broken by summed option probability) of two 384-token or five 128-token samples score 0.05--0.19 below one 768-token sample.
